25312 #edb/ref 1061 11376 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125312 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "74d5fcb5-d32b-5ddd-92cd-491ff00e4b54" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125312 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124467 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125313 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125314 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125313 #edb/ref 1061 11379 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125313 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "147b9868-60b3-5d25-8663-966137fb6540" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125313 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124468 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125314 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125315 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125314 #edb/ref 1061 11383 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125314 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "81eef922-1c17-502c-89a2-3e132f4a64e6" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125314 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125746 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125315 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125316 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125315 #edb/ref 1061 11384 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125315 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "20b0d4d3-7059-5c01-9a72-d14629d41a1c" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125315 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124469 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125316 #edb/ref 1061 11387 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125316 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "266600d8-59f0-586e-ba73-083efb5b7259" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125316 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124470 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125741 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a function $z=f(x,y)$, if both partial derivatives $f_x,$ $f_y$ exist and are continuous in a neighborhood of a point $P(x_0,y_0)$ in the domain of the function, and if $\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0) \\neq \\mathbf 0,$ then we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $C$ that passes through the point $P.$*\n\nThis property is one of the most important facts in multivariable calculus since it gives a fundamental link between calculus and geometry!\n\nLet's illustrate this property with a concrete example. Consider the paraboloid $z = f (x, y)= x^2 + y^2$ and the level curve $\\color{red}C$ of $f(x,y)$ corresponding to the point $P(1,-1).$ It is easy to see that $\\color{red}C$ is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ with equation $x^2+y^2 = 2.$\n\n![](images/f2/f2461dbfcde2403e6ed7de9d6288d33a4deb52319d35b7036628c3fa8b3789dc.png)\n\nIn this particular instance, the property says that\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(1,-1)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $\\color{red}C$ that passes through the point $P(1,-1).$*\n\n![](images/48/481b68af522b9144e232d9c775f734cd998b31a21571cbfd983c23d0f68c3688.png)\n\nLooking at the situation in the $xy$-plane, we see the following:\n\n![](images/ce/ceb353d5164d6aa089a37e77c9469d747ef4430e363e42f9d35ee6d769d9a4e4.png)\n\nLet's verify that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is a normal line to the level curve $C$ at the point $P(1,-1).$\n\n- First, computing the gradient, we find that $\\nabla f = \\langle 2x, 2y \\rangle.$ Therefore, $$\\nabla f(1,-1) = \\langle 2, -2 \\rangle.$$\n- Then, using elementary calculus, we can show that the equation of the tangent to $C$ at $P$ is $y=x-2.$ Since this line has slope $m=1,$ it follows that the vector $\\langle 1,1 \\rangle$ is parallel to the level curve.\n- Finally, taking the dot product between the gradient and the tangent vector, we obtain $$\\nabla f(1,-1)\\cdot \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = \\langle 2, -2\\rangle \\cdot \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = 0.$$\n\nThis means that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is *perpendicular* to the tangent line to $C$ at $(1,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125741 #edb/ref 1065 2499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125741 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "68a96106-e224-5989-af04-8d464b6e58d5" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125741 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125746 #edb/ref 1064 "The same property holds true in the case of three or more variables. In general, if $f$ is a differentiable real-valued function of $n$ variables, $\\mathbf y$ is a point in the domain of $f,$ and if the gradient vector is nonzero, i.e.,\n\n$$\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)= \\left \\langle f_{x_1}(\\mathbf y), f_{x_2}(\\mathbf y), \\cdots, f_{x_n}(\\mathbf y)\\right \\rangle \\neq \\mathbf 0,$$\n\nthen we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)$ is a normal vector to the level surface $f(x_1,x_2, \\cdots, x_n) =f(\\mathbf y)$ at $\\mathbf y.$*\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function\n\n$$w = f (x, y, z)= \\dfrac 1 2\\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2-1\\right).$$\n\nThe level surface corresponding to $w=0$ is the sphere $\\mathcal S$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 +z^2 =1.$\n\nThe point $P(0,0,1)$ lies on $\\mathcal S.$ Computing $\\nabla f(0,0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\\eqalign{ \\nabla f(x,y,z) &= \\langle f_x, \\: f_y, \\:f_z\\rangle \\[5pt] &= \\dfrac12\\langle2x, \\: 2y, \\: 2z\\rangle\\[5pt] &= \\langle x, \\: y, \\: z\\rangle\\[10pt] \\nabla f(0,0,1) &= \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 1 \\rangle. }$$\n\nNow clearly, $\\nabla f (0,0, 1)$ is perpendicular to $\\mathcal S$ at the point $(0,0,1).$\n\n![](images/88/88bf25de31ae3318379865cdf2590c15216cb984cbf3948f975caa47317be664.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125746 #edb/ref 1065 2524 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125746 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "86223826-30df-5eba-9375-355401199095" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186125746 #edb/ref 1067 "The Gradient as a Normal Vector For Functions of More than Two Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263011 #edb/ref 1081 "2x-y-5=0" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263011 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263011 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "0029eb5c-a868-5b35-b213-3fa6e3a04a1d" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263390 #edb/ref 1081 "x-3y-5=0" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263390 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263390 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "2d9853cb-1b4d-5d99-b740-f207d47a13c3" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263860 #edb/ref 1081 "x+y-1=0" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263860 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263860 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "62cb3b0d-c5e0-5140-9b14-0b73eecbc9ac" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263982 #edb/ref 1081 "x-2y-4=0" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263982 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186263982 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "70e32100-046e-5e0e-b9f2-eac7fe211b6f" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186264581 #edb/ref 1081 "x+2y=0" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186264581 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186264581 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "b5d5900d-9aee-5b56-aa97-4e95d9c8f10a" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265322 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263011 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265322 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263390 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265322 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263860 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265322 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263982 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265322 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186264581 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265322 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265322 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265322 #edb/ref 1079 #edb/ref 17592186263011 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265322 #edb/ref 1080 #uuid "263638a6-9832-58e0-a5f7-da0d13d29e2d" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265967 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265322 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265967 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130723" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265967 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265967 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the plane curve $4xy+2{x}^{2}=x+2y.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265967 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d42d4bf9-4eef-57b6-b4ed-d5448bfe2b61" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186265967 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4xy+2{x}^{2} & =x+2y \\\\ 4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(4y+4x-1)\\,i+(4x-2)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =(4(-1)+4(2)-1)\\,i+(4(2)-2)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨3,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Hence, $∇f(2,-1)=⟨3,6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{6} \\\\ 6(x-2) & =3(y+1) \\\\ 2(x-2) & =y+1 \\\\ 2x-4 & =y+1 \\\\ 2x-y-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406804 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406819 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406804 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406820 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406804 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186406821 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406804 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406804 #edb/ref 1045 "The Gradient as a Normal Vector" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406804 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186406819 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406804 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046665 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406804 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050033 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406804 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "70dc8b50-8f87-5b55-9f2e-265dbd1adbfc" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406805 #edb/ref 1081 "x+y-3=0" #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406805 #edb/ref 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045585] [17592186406805 #edb/ref 1084 #uuid "1a1d5e01-e81c-5544-a92